---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import catalogue from '../data/matchers.json';

// Generated from ci_doctor/config/defaults.yml by `mise run docs:data`; a test
// fails if this file drifts from what ships. Never edit the JSON by hand.
const total = catalogue.reduce((n, g) => n + g.packs.length, 0);

const addPack = `# .ci-doctor.yml — a new id is *added* to the shipped packs
extraction:
  matchers:
    - id: my_service_errors
      pattern: '^\\[myservice\\] (ERROR|PANIC)'
      before: 3
      after: 12
      priority: 85`;

const retune = `# A shipped id *overrides* that pack, field by field, and logs a warning.
extraction:
  matchers:
    - id: pytest
      priority: 95        # start:/end: are inherited, not blanked`;

const testIt = `# Prove it fires before you trust it — a matcher that never matches
# fails silently, because the tail window still returns something.
ci-doctor analyze path/to/failing-job.log`;
---
<Base title="Matchers" description="The shipped matcher packs — what each one anchors on, and how to add or retune your own.">
  <h1>Matchers</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A matcher recognises one tool's failure output and pulls the lines around it into the
    evidence. {total} ship by default, covering the test frameworks, compilers, linters and
    package managers most pipelines run. They are data, not code — every one is overridable
    from your own <code>.ci-doctor.yml</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>How a pack is shaped</h2>
  <p>There are two forms, and a pack uses one or the other:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Fields</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Anchor</strong></td>
        <td><code>pattern</code> + <code>before</code>/<code>after</code></td>
        <td>The failure is one recognisable line, and the useful context is a known number of lines around it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Block</strong></td>
        <td><code>start</code> + <code>end</code></td>
        <td>The tool prints a delimited region — <code>=== FAILURES ===</code> through to the summary line.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Both carry a <code>priority</code>, which decides who survives when the evidence exceeds
    the token budget: whole low-priority windows are shed before the survivors are trimmed.
    Rank a pack by how <em>diagnostic</em> it is, not how loud — <code>npm ERR!</code> (75)
    always trails the compiler errors that caused it, so it loses to <code>tsc</code> (80).
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Anchor on the cause, not the summary.</strong> A tool's final
    <code>2 tests failed</code> line comes <em>after</em> the detail explaining why, so a
    summary-only anchor windows straight past the evidence. Anchor on something only your
    tool prints, too: a bare <code>^ERROR: .*failed</code> also matches
    <code>ERROR: Job failed: exit code 1</code>, the line that ends every failed GitLab job.
  </div>

  <h2>The shipped catalogue</h2>
  <p>
    Generated from <code>config/defaults.yml</code>, so this table is the packs that actually
    ship. <code>before</code>/<code>after</code> are context lines; a blank means zero.
  </p>

  {catalogue.map((group) => (
    <>
      <h3>{group.group}</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>id</th>
            <th>Anchors on</th>
            <th>Ends at</th>
            <th>±</th>
            <th>Pri</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {group.packs.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{p.id}</code></td>
              <td><code class="rx">{p.match}</code></td>
              <td>{p.end ? <code class="rx">{p.end}</code> : <span class="dash">—</span>}</td>
              <td class="ctx">{p.before || p.after ? `${p.before}/${p.after}` : <span class="dash">—</span>}</td>
              <td class="pri">{p.priority}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2>Adding your own</h2>
  <p>
    Matcher lists merge <strong>by id</strong>, which is the one exception to "lists replace".
    A new id is added alongside the shipped packs:
  </p>
  <Code code={addPack} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />

  <p>Reusing a shipped id retunes that pack instead, field by field:</p>
  <Code code={retune} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />
  <p>
    Only the fields you write are overridden — setting <code>priority</code> on
    <code>pytest</code> keeps its <code>start</code>/<code>end</code>, because blanking them
    would leave a matcher that can never fire. ci-doctor logs a warning naming any shipped
    pack you override, so an accidental id collision is visible rather than silent.
  </p>

  <h2>Check it actually fires</h2>
  <p>
    The most common mistake is a pack that never matches. It fails <em>silently</em>: the
    tail window still returns output, so the report looks fine while your matcher does
    nothing. Replay a real failing log and confirm your lines are in the evidence:
  </p>
  <Code code={testIt} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />
  <p>
    Contributing a pack upstream? It needs a fixture log for <em>both</em> providers plus an
    expected verdict — see <a href="https://github.com/fennet82/ci-doctor/blob/master/GUIDELINES.md">GUIDELINES.md</a> §5.1.
    The <a href={url('/concepts/')}>concepts</a> page explains where extraction sits in the
    pipeline.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Regexes are the content here, so let them wrap rather than scroll the row. */
  code.rx {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    word-break: break-word;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .ctx,
  .pri {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .pri { color: var(--muted); }
  .dash { color: var(--muted); }

  /* The catalogue is many small tables; tighten the default h3 rhythm. */
  h3 { margin-top: 2.25rem; }
</style>
